  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 AM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... After dry weather today and only a slight chance of \nshowers and storms in the far west on Saturday, moisture and \nprobability of convection will increase on Sunday and continue into \nat least early next week.\n\nThere is high confidence that a positively-tilted mid/upper trough \nwill approach from the OH/TN Valleys this weekend. While the trough \nshould still be far enough west to keep much of central NC dry on \nSaturday outside of the far western Piedmont where POPs are slight \nchance, it will slowly make its way east on Sunday and Monday, with \ndeep SSW flow ahead of it bringing increasing moisture (PW values of \n2-2.5 inches, or 125-150% of normal). Despite widespread cloud \ncover, high temperatures in the 80s and dew points in the 70s should \nstill allow for weak to moderate instability to develop. And by \nMonday, embedded upper impulses and possible upper divergence from a \njet over the Appalachians may provide some forcing for ascent. All \nof these ingredients should result in widespread showers and storms \nfrom Sunday into Monday night, and ensemble mean QPF is generally in \nthe 0.5-1.5 inch range on average during this time. Locally higher \namounts are possible, and WPC includes our region in a marginal \n(level 1 of 4) risk for excessive rainfall on Monday. This certainly \nprovides an opportunity for more relief from the ongoing drought.\n\nDespite good model agreement on widespread rainfall on \nSunday/Monday, there are still major differences between the global \nmodels on how the trough evolves. The GFS continues to keep its \nsouthern portion stronger and closes off a low over the OH Valley \nand Central Appalachians by Monday, and the 00z CMC does something \nsimilar. This solution would result in stronger mid-level flow and a \npossible severe storm threat, compared to the ECMWF which does not \nhave a closed low over the OH Valley and thus has weaker flow \noverall.\n\nThe trough will lift NE and deamplify from Tuesday onward, but \ncontinued moist SW flow should result in above-normal shower and \nstorm chances through much of the workweek, albeit gradually \ndecreasing each day. This will keep temperatures in check with highs \nmostly in the 80s.",
